Task 1: Monitoring Processes with top and ps
Subtask 1.1: Using the top command
Open a terminal and run:

top
Observe the real-time system summary:

CPU usage (us: user, sy: system, ni: nice, id: idle)
Memory usage (total, free, used, buff/cache)
Process list sorted by CPU usage by default
While in top:

Press M to sort by memory usage
Press P to return to CPU sorting
Press q to quit
Subtask 1.2: Using ps for process analysis
To list all running processes with CPU and memory info:
ps aux
To find the top 5 CPU-consuming processes:
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6
To find the top 5 memory-consuming processes:
ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -6
Expected Output:
You should see a list of processes with their PID, CPU%, MEM%, and command details.

Troubleshooting Tip:
If ps shows incomplete output, try widening your terminal or piping to less:

ps aux | less
Task 2: Terminating Processes
Subtask 2.1: Graceful termination with kill
Identify a process to terminate (use pidof or pgrep):
pidof firefox
or
pgrep -l firefox
Send SIGTERM (15) for graceful shutdown:
kill <PID>
Verify the process is gone:
ps -p <PID>
Subtask 2.2: Forceful termination
If the process doesn't respond to SIGTERM:
kill -9 <PID>
(SIGKILL/9 cannot be ignored by processes)
Subtask 2.3: Using killall
To kill all instances of a program:
killall chrome
For forceful kill:
killall -9 chrome
Warning:
Be cautious with kill -9 and killall as they can cause data loss or system instability.

Task 3: Process Priority Adjustment
Subtask 3.1: Launching processes with nice
Start a process with low priority (higher nice value):
nice -n 19 tar -czf backup.tar.gz /large_directory
(Nice values range from -20 [highest priority] to 19 [lowest])
Subtask 3.2: Adjusting running processes with renice
Find a running CPU-intensive process:
top
Note its PID and current nice value (NI column)
Change its priority:
sudo renice -n 10 -p <PID>
Verify the change in top
Note:
Only root can increase priority (lower nice value) of existing processes
